{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0116kXQxJfo4M13ro9aCZPLt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Important observations:\n1. `-s` doesn't resolve symlinks — `/tmp/rpt/link` stays as `/tmp/rpt/link` (not `/tmp/rpt/sub/file`). It also doesn't resolve `/tmp` → `/private/tmp`.\n2. Without `-s`, symlinks are resolved: `/tmp/rpt/link` → `/private/tmp/rpt/sub/file`\n3. `-s -m` with missing path: `/tmp/rpt/nodir/nofile` stays as-is (no symlink resolution)\n\n\n4. The `--relative-to` flag makes paths relative to a base directory, while `--relative-base` only applies relative formatting if the path starts with that base — otherwise it returns the absolute path.\n5. When both flags are used together, `--relative-to` takes precedence for the relative conversion, but `--relative-base` still controls whether to return absolute or relative depending on path matching.\n6. I'm realizing that both the `--relative-to` and `--relative-base` values themselves get resolved to their canonical paths before comparison, so `/tmp` becomes `/private/tmp`, and the relative calculation happens between these resolved paths.\n\nNow I need to figure out how the `-s` flag (which prevents symlink resolution) interacts with these relative path options.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":486,"cache_read_input_tokens":32214,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":486},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ee281473-7df6-40c9-835f-ac74196a76ca","uuid":"f0a79734-32da-4927-b82c-fc0303f0e3da"}
