{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SoJYeL3KmUevuXpLnCpDJp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Key findings:\n1. `-s` with `--relative-to=/tmp` → `rpt/link` — the `-s` path `/tmp/rpt/link` stays as-is, then relative to `/tmp` (which with `-s` also stays as `/tmp`, not resolved to `/private/tmp`)\n2. `-s` with `--relative-base=/tmp /var` → `/var` — with `-s`, `/var` stays as `/var` (not resolved to `/private/var`), and since it doesn't start with `/tmp`, absolute printed\n\nSo the `--relative-to` and `--relative-base` DIR values are ALSO subject to the same resolution mode. \n\nWhen `-s` is active, the DIR values are cleaned but not symlink-resolved. Now I need to refactor the implementation to use a config struct that bundles the resolve mode, strip flag, and directory options together, then update parseArgs to handle the short and long flag variants while returning both the config and operands, and finally adjust the resolve function to apply the same resolution logic to the directory parameters.\n\nI'm also thinking through how `-s` behaves across the different modes—in default mode it should just clean the path without resolving symlinks or checking intermediate components, but I should verify what the actual behavior is for missing intermediate directories.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1178,"cache_read_input_tokens":32700,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1178},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ee281473-7df6-40c9-835f-ac74196a76ca","uuid":"7ecdaf43-0a13-435a-b8b9-9dd807c8fffc"}
